---
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro'
import { company, contact, googlePolicies, site } from '../site'

const sections = [
  { id: 'scope', title: 'Who we are and what this policy covers' },
  { id: 'information-we-collect', title: 'Information we collect' },
  { id: 'how-we-use-information', title: 'How we use information' },
  { id: 'google-user-data', title: 'Google user data' },
  { id: 'microsoft-calendar-data', title: 'Microsoft 365 calendar data' },
  { id: 'ai-assistants', title: 'AI assistants and API access' },
  { id: 'sharing', title: 'How we share information' },
  { id: 'invitees', title: 'If you book a meeting through Hourmere' },
  { id: 'self-hosted', title: 'Self-hosted Hourmere' },
  { id: 'retention', title: 'Retention and deletion' },
  { id: 'security', title: 'Security' },
  { id: 'international-transfers', title: 'International transfers' },
  { id: 'your-rights', title: 'Your rights and choices' },
  { id: 'this-website', title: 'This website, cookies and local storage' },
  { id: 'children', title: 'Children' },
  { id: 'changes', title: 'Changes to this policy' },
  { id: 'contact', title: 'Contact' },
]

const privacyMail = `mailto:${contact.privacy}`
---

<Legal
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How Hourmere, operated by Cobaltmere, LLC, collects, uses, stores, shares and deletes personal data, including data from Google Calendar and Microsoft 365."
  path="/privacy"
  effective="2026-10-15"
  sections={sections}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      This Privacy Policy explains how {company.name} ("{company.shortName}", "we", "us") collects,
      uses, stores, shares and deletes personal data in connection with Hourmere: the website at
      hourmere.com, the Hourmere cloud service that we host ("Hourmere Cloud"), and its API and
      remote MCP server (together, the "Service").
    </p>
    <p class="callout">
      Hourmere is in development and Hourmere Cloud has not launched yet. This policy describes how
      we will handle personal data once it does. Until then, the only personal data we receive
      through Hourmere is the website request data described in
      <a href="#this-website">section 14</a> and any messages you choose to send us.
    </p>
    <section class="summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
      <h2 id="summary-title">The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We use your data to run Hourmere and to provide the scheduling features you use.</li>
        <li>
          Calendar data from Google or Microsoft is used only to show your availability and manage
          your bookings. We never sell it, never use it for advertising and never use it to train
          generalized AI or machine learning models.
        </li>
        <li>AI assistants can access your account only after you authorize them.</li>
        <li>If you self-host Hourmere, your data stays on your server and we do not receive it.</li>
        <li>You can disconnect calendars and export or delete your data at any time.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="scope">1. Who we are and what this policy covers</h2>
  <p>
    Hourmere is open-source scheduling software. It is developed and operated by {company.name}, a
    Delaware limited liability company. For Hourmere Cloud, {company.shortName} is responsible for
    the personal data described in this policy, except where we process data on behalf of our
    customers as described in <a href="#invitees">section 8</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    This policy does not cover Hourmere installations that other people or organizations run on
    their own servers (see <a href="#self-hosted">section 9</a>), or services from third parties
    that you connect to Hourmere, such as your calendar provider or your AI assistant. The company
    website at cobaltmere.com has its own privacy policy.
  </p>

  <h2 id="information-we-collect">2. Information we collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Account information:</strong> your name, email address, time zone and settings, and
      the credentials you use to sign in. If you sign in with a password, we store it only in hashed
      form.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Calendar connection information:</strong> when you connect a Google or Microsoft
      calendar, the access and refresh tokens the provider issues to Hourmere, and the email address
      of the connected account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Calendar data:</strong> free/busy information and the event data needed to work out
      your availability and to create and manage events for your bookings. See <a
        href="#google-user-data">section 4</a
      > and <a href="#microsoft-calendar-data">section 5</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Scheduling information:</strong> the event types, availability settings and booking pages
      you set up.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Booking information:</strong> details that invitees enter when they book with you,
      such as their name, email address and notes, and the time, time zone and status of each
      booking.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>API and agent access:</strong> the API keys you create and the OAuth authorizations you
      grant to AI assistants and other applications, and records of the requests they make.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Billing information</strong> (paid plans only): your plan, number of seats, billing
      country and transaction records. Card details are collected and processed by Stripe; we do not
      store full card numbers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Usage and diagnostic information:</strong> log data such as IP address, browser and
      device type, the pages and API endpoints requested, timestamps and error reports.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Communications:</strong> messages you send to us, for example to our support address.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="how-we-use-information">3. How we use information</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      To provide the Service: computing your availability, showing your booking pages, creating,
      updating and cancelling calendar events for bookings, sending email confirmations and
      reminders, and carrying out requests from applications you have authorized.
    </li>
    <li>To keep the Service secure, prevent fraud and abuse, and find and fix problems.</li>
    <li>To process payments for paid plans.</li>
    <li>To answer your questions and send you service-related notices.</li>
    <li>
      To understand how the Service is used and improve it, using usage and diagnostic information.
    </li>
    <li>To comply with legal obligations and enforce our terms.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We do not sell personal data. We do not use personal data for advertising, and we do not use
    calendar data or booking information to train generalized AI or machine learning models. We send
    marketing emails only if you ask to receive them.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are in the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom or Switzerland, our legal bases
    for processing are: performance of our contract with you; our legitimate interests in
    operating, securing and improving the Service; compliance with legal obligations; and your
    consent where we ask for it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="google-user-data">4. Google user data</h2>
  <p>
    This section describes how Hourmere accesses, uses, stores, shares and deletes data it receives
    from Google when you connect Google Calendar ("Google user data"). Connecting Google Calendar is
    optional; Hourmere asks for access only when you choose to connect it.
  </p>

  <h3>What we access</h3>
  <p>
    Depending on the permissions you grant on Google's consent screen, Hourmere accesses:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the email address of your Google account, to show you which account is connected;</li>
    <li>
      the list of calendars in your account (such as their names, identifiers and time zones), so
      you can choose which calendars to check for conflicts and which calendar receives new
      bookings;
    </li>
    <li>free/busy information for the calendars you select;</li>
    <li>
      event data needed for scheduling, such as the start and end times and status of events on the
      calendars you select, so Hourmere can work out when you are available; and
    </li>
    <li>
      the events Hourmere creates on your calendar for bookings, including the details written to
      them (such as the title, time, description and attendees), so that Hourmere can update or
      remove them when a booking is rescheduled or cancelled.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We request only the permissions these features need. Google's consent screen shows the exact
    permissions before you agree.
  </p>

  <h3>How we use it</h3>
  <p>
    We use Google user data only to provide and improve Hourmere's user-facing scheduling features:
    showing your available times on your booking pages and to applications you authorize,
    preventing double-booking, adding booked meetings to your calendar, updating or removing those
    events when bookings change, and showing you the status of your calendar connection.
  </p>
  <p>We do not:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>sell Google user data;</li>
    <li>
      use or transfer it for advertising, including retargeting and personalized or interest-based
      advertising;
    </li>
    <li>transfer it to advertising platforms, data brokers or information resellers;</li>
    <li>use it to determine credit-worthiness or for lending purposes; or</li>
    <li>
      use or transfer it to develop, improve or train generalized or non-personalized AI or machine
      learning models.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How we share it</h3>
  <p>We do not share or transfer Google user data, except:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      as needed to provide the features you use. For example, people who visit your booking page
      see the times you are available, but not the details of your calendar events, and an AI
      assistant or other application you authorize receives the results of the requests it makes
      for you (see <a href="#ai-assistants">section 6</a>);
    </li>
    <li>
      with service providers that host and run the Service for us, such as our cloud hosting and
      database providers, who may process it only on our instructions and only to provide the
      Service;
    </li>
    <li>when necessary for security purposes, such as investigating abuse;</li>
    <li>to comply with applicable laws; or</li>
    <li>
      as part of a merger, acquisition or sale of assets, only after obtaining your explicit prior
      consent.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Who can read it</h3>
  <p>
    Our staff do not read your Google user data unless you give us explicit permission for specific
    data (for example, to help with a support request), it is necessary for security purposes (such
    as investigating a bug or abuse), it is necessary to comply with applicable law, or the data
    has been aggregated and anonymized and is used for internal operations in line with applicable
    law.
  </p>

  <h3>How we store and protect it</h3>
  <p>
    Google user data is stored on servers operated by our cloud hosting providers in the United
    States and/or the European Union. It is encrypted in transit. Calendar connection tokens are
    stored encrypted. Access is limited to the systems and personnel that need it to run the
    Service. See <a href="#security">section 11</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Retention and deletion</h3>
  <p>
    We keep Google user data only as long as we need it to provide the features described above.
    Calendar data that Hourmere stores to compute availability is refreshed and replaced as your
    calendar changes. When you disconnect Google Calendar, Hourmere stops accessing it and deletes
    the connection tokens and the calendar data it stored from that connection within 30 days.
    Events that Hourmere has already created on your calendar stay there, because they are part of
    your calendar. When you delete your Hourmere account, we delete your Google user data within 30
    days.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can also remove Hourmere's access at any time from your Google Account's <a
      href={googlePolicies.connections}>third-party connections page</a
    >, and you can ask us to delete your data by emailing <a href={privacyMail}>{contact.privacy}</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h3>Limited Use</h3>
  <p class="callout">
    Hourmere's use and transfer to any other app of information received from Google APIs will
    adhere to the <a href={googlePolicies.userData}>Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
    including the Limited Use requirements. Hourmere's use of information received from Google
    Workspace APIs, including the Google Calendar API, also adheres to the <a
      href={googlePolicies.workspace}>Google Workspace API User Data and Developer Policy</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    If we change how Hourmere uses Google user data, we will tell you and ask for your consent
    before using it in a new way or for a different purpose.
  </p>

  <h2 id="microsoft-calendar-data">5. Microsoft 365 calendar data</h2>
  <p>
    When you connect a Microsoft 365 calendar, Hourmere accesses the same kinds of data
    described for Google in section 4: the connected account's email address, your calendar list,
    free/busy information and the event data needed to compute availability, and the events it
    creates for bookings. We use, share, protect and delete Microsoft calendar data in the same way
    and under the same restrictions: only to provide scheduling features, never sold, never used for
    advertising and never used to train generalized AI or machine learning models. You can remove
    Hourmere's access from your Microsoft account's app permissions, or ask your organization's
    administrator.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-assistants">6. AI assistants and API access</h2>
  <p>
    Hourmere has an API and a remote MCP server that AI assistants and other applications can use.
    They can access your account only after you authorize them, by creating an API key or by
    approving an OAuth request. An authorized application can do what the API allows: list your
    event types, check your availability, and book, reschedule or cancel meetings.
  </p>
  <p>
    The availability Hourmere returns is calculated from your connected calendars. When an
    application you authorized makes a request, we send it the results at your direction. That
    application and the AI model behind it are operated by their own providers, and their handling
    of data is governed by those providers' terms and privacy policies, not this one. You can revoke an
    API key or an authorization at any time.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sharing">7. How we share information</h2>
  <p>
    We do not sell personal data. We share it only as described in this policy, including with:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Service providers</strong> that help us run the Service under contracts that require
      them to protect it and use it only on our instructions: cloud hosting and database providers
      in the United States and/or the European Union, an email delivery provider for booking
      confirmations and reminders, and Stripe for payment processing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Calendar providers you connect</strong> (Google and Microsoft), to read your
      availability and to create, update and remove events for your bookings.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Applications you authorize</strong>, such as AI assistants, as described in <a
        href="#ai-assistants">section 6</a
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The other party to a booking.</strong> When someone books with you, the details they
      enter are shared with you, and your name and the booking details are shared with them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Legal and safety reasons:</strong> when required by law, or to protect the rights,
      property or safety of our users, the public or {company.shortName}.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Business transfers:</strong> if {company.shortName} is involved in a merger,
      acquisition or sale of assets, subject to this policy. For Google user data, we will first
      obtain your explicit consent.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="invitees">8. If you book a meeting through Hourmere</h2>
  <p>
    If you book a meeting on someone's Hourmere booking page, the person or organization you are
    booking with (the "host") decides what information to ask for and how to use it. We process
    your booking information on the host's behalf to provide the Service, for example to add the
    meeting to the host's calendar and send you confirmations and reminders. For questions about
    how a host uses your information, contact the host. If you contact us, we will pass your
    request on or help where we can.
  </p>

  <h2 id="self-hosted">9. Self-hosted Hourmere</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone can run the open-source Hourmere software on their own servers. A self-hosted instance is
    operated by whoever runs it, and that operator is responsible for the data it holds and for its
    connections to calendar providers. {company.shortName} does not receive data from self-hosted
    instances. If we ever add telemetry to the software, it will be optional, opt-in and anonymous,
    and we will document what it sends.
  </p>

  <h2 id="retention">10. Retention and deletion</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      We keep account, scheduling and booking information while your account is active. You can
      delete event types, bookings and calendar connections at any time.
    </li>
    <li>
      When you delete your account, we delete your data, including booking information about your
      invitees, within 30 days, except records we must keep for legal, tax or accounting purposes.
      Those records never include calendar data.
    </li>
    <li>
      Usage and diagnostic logs are kept for a limited period for security and troubleshooting, and
      then deleted.
    </li>
    <li>Google user data is retained and deleted as described in section 4.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="security">11. Security</h2>
  <p>
    We protect personal data with measures appropriate to the risk, including encryption in transit,
    encrypted storage of calendar connection tokens, access controls and the principle of least
    privilege. No system is completely secure. If a data breach affects your personal data, we will
    notify you as required by law. To report a security issue, email <a
      href={`mailto:${contact.security}`}>{contact.security}</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id="international-transfers">12. International transfers</h2>
  <p>
    We are based in the United States, and our cloud hosting providers store data in the United
    States and/or the European Union. Where the law requires it, we protect international transfers
    of personal data with appropriate safeguards, such as the European Commission's Standard
    Contractual Clauses.
  </p>

  <h2 id="your-rights">13. Your rights and choices</h2>
  <p>
    Depending on where you live, you may have the right to access, correct, delete or export your
    personal data, to restrict or object to its processing, and to withdraw consent at any time. You
    can disconnect calendars, revoke API keys and authorizations, and delete your account in the
    Service. To exercise any of these rights, email <a href={privacyMail}>{contact.privacy}</a>. We
    will not discriminate against you for exercising them. You may also lodge a complaint with your
    local data protection authority.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not sell personal information or share it for cross-context behavioral advertising, as
    those terms are defined under California law.
  </p>

  <h2 id="this-website">14. This website, cookies and local storage</h2>
  <p>
    hourmere.com is a static website. It does not set cookies or use analytics or third-party trackers,
    and its fonts are served from hourmere.com. Our website hosting provider processes standard
    request data, such as IP address, browser type and the page requested, to deliver the site and
    protect it from abuse. The documentation pages save small preferences, such as your light or
    dark theme choice, in your browser's local storage; this stays on your device.
  </p>
  <p>
    When Hourmere Cloud launches, it will use only cookies that are strictly necessary for it to
    work, such as keeping you signed in. We do not use advertising cookies.
  </p>

  <h2 id="children">15. Children</h2>
  <p>
    The Service is not directed to children under 16, and we do not knowingly collect their personal
    data. If you believe a child has given us personal data, contact us and we will delete it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">16. Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    We may update this policy. We will post the new version on this page with a new effective date
    and, if the changes are material, notify you by email or in the Service before they take effect.
    Changes to how we use Google user data are also subject to section 4.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact">17. Contact</h2>
  <p>
    {company.name}<br />
    Privacy questions and requests: <a href={privacyMail}>{contact.privacy}</a><br />
    Support: <a href={`mailto:${contact.support}`}>{contact.support}</a><br />
    Security reports: <a href={`mailto:${contact.security}`}>{contact.security}</a><br />
    Website: <a href={site.url}>hourmere.com</a> · <a href={company.url}>cobaltmere.com</a>
  </p>
</Legal>
